Antidepressants work for approximately 50% of patients on the first prescription; exercise offers an alternative pathway that works through different neurochemical mechanisms.
The WHO's treatment guidelines for depression now include structured exercise as a first-line intervention for mild-to-moderate cases.
Relapse rates after exercise-based treatment are lower than after medication-only treatment, with one Duke study showing 6-month relapse rates of 8% vs 38%.
Group exercise provides a social dimension that independently benefits depression — addressing both biochemistry and isolation simultaneously.
Exercise has no negative side effects, is free or nearly free, and is available globally — making it a uniquely equitable mental health intervention.
